-- An `==` whose derived answer is SHARED with an earlier one on the same
-- receiver (checker-v2.md §9.5's memo: `alias` of the first), when the
-- first then fails, is one `not_equatable` and nothing else
-- (a regression guard). The two `p == p`
-- share `p`'s derived tuple answer; `apply x` makes `x` a function after
-- both were answered, so the first's element fails and the second is left
-- `answered`, an alias whose chain ends in a failed wanted. Elaboration
-- (P6) follows the chain to its end and treats it as failed: the module
-- has its message, so no evidence is written and no `internal` is added.
apply : (Int → Int) → Int
apply f = f 1


h x =
    p = ( x, 1 )
    if p == p then
        p == p && apply x == 0
    else
        False
